{"id":5913,"date":"2022-03-02T14:00:53","date_gmt":"2022-03-02T22:00:53","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=5913"},"modified":"2025-10-23T13:39:23","modified_gmt":"2025-10-23T20:39:23","slug":"3-4-gyroscope-cde-blockly","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/3-4-gyroscope-cde-blockly\/","title":{"rendered":"3.4: Gyroscope"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221378243af\" name=\"6a221378243af\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>We've Moved!<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text css=&#8221;.vc_custom_1761251960983{padding-top: 20px !important;padding-right: 20px !important;padding-bottom: 20px !important;padding-left: 20px !important;background-color: #ffdddd !important;border-radius: 3px !important;}&#8221;]<\/p>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" class=\"alignleft wp-image-11766\" src=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-content\/uploads\/2023\/06\/icon_warning-150x150-2-84x85.png\" alt=\"\" width=\"81\" height=\"82\" \/><\/p>\n<p data-pm-slice=\"1 1 []\"><strong>We\u2019ve moved to our new lesson portal: <a href=\"http:\/\/learn.robolink.com\">Robolink Learn<\/a>! Lessons here will remain available until the end of June 2026. This version of Basecamp will no longer be updated and uses outdated references to Blockly for Robolink. You can access the previous version of <a href=\"https:\/\/codrone.robolink.com\/edu\/blockly-archive\/\">Blockly here<\/a>.<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221378244ee\" name=\"6a221378244ee\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Goals and Steps to Success<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Goals:<\/p>\n<p>By the end of this lesson, I will be able to:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Describe how a gyroscope works<\/li>\n<li>Explain how a gyroscope reports movements<\/li>\n<li>Explain the difference between displacement and angular rotation<\/li>\n<li>Describe how gyroscope values can be used to improve the accuracy of flight movements<\/li>\n<li>Create programs that use gyroscope values to control flight movements<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Steps to Success:<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>What is a Gyroscope?<\/li>\n<li>Angular Rotation and Displacement<\/li>\n<li>Getting Gyroscope Angles<\/li>\n<li>Put it in a Loop!<\/li>\n<li>Turn 90 Degrees<\/li>\n<li>Gyro with LED<\/li>\n<li>Turn_degree()<\/li>\n<li>Challenge 1: A More Perfect Polygon<\/li>\n<li>Challenge 2: Plinko<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22137824558\" name=\"6a22137824558\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>What is a Gyroscope?<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]When you think of a <strong>gyroscope<\/strong>, you may be thinking of a mounted, spinning wheel that stays balanced in the same orientation, no matter which direction you turn or flip it. <em>How?<\/em> This can be explained by conservation of momentum. While you may not have heard of this term before, you have experienced this. For example, if you have ever spun around on an office chair, you probably noticed you spin faster when you bring your arms and legs in. Since momentum has to be conserved, your speed increases when you bring your mass (arms and legs) closer to you.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;1971&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<p class=\"highlight-line\" data-source-line=\"32\">In electronics, gyroscopes don\u2019t look like this. They are tiny chips that also use motion to detect changes in orientation. They use a concept called the Coriolis Effect by vibrating a plate on top of a chip. When the phone or device moves, it causes the vibrating disc to slide. This movement changes the electrical current transmitted to the sensor, which is then translated into a digital value. We know it\u2019s hard to believe, but the tiny microchips you see on the CoDrone EDU have moving parts that are affected by movement!<\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221378245a9\" name=\"6a221378245a9\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>What Does the Gyroscope Measure?<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Gyroscopes are necessary to stabilize your drone while flying, since many factors could cause your drone to drift off course. A gyroscope can measure how far and in which direction your drone is tilting or rotating. Using some math and algorithms, a drone can try and correct any unwanted displacements to remain stable. Displacement is a movement from one position to another position.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;7758&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]There are three orientations that we are concerned with when we are flying a drone for stability: roll, pitch, and yaw. The gyroscope sensor in your CoDrone will return values from 0 to 180 degrees, positive or negative, that correspond to the degrees the drone rotated in each axis. If you have trouble remembering which way is positive and which way is negative, think of the flight movements![\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221378245f7\" name=\"6a221378245f7\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Getting Gyroscope Angles<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]You may notice there are two blocks related to the gyroscope. For this example, use the function\u00a0<strong><code>get_angle()<\/code><\/strong> in the &#8220;Sensors&#8221; menu.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;13186&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]In a 3-axis gyroscope, you have access to all 3 axes. Add three blocks and use the dropdown menu to select the axes. Finally, connect a<strong> print<\/strong> block to each one.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;10608&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a2213782465b\" name=\"6a2213782465b\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Put it in a Loop<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<p class=\"\" data-source-line=\"56\">It is not helpful to take one snapshot of the angle data because you can\u2019t see how the angles change. Set up a for loop that will read the drone\u2019s angles 100 times. This sounds like a lot, but this will give enough time to rotate the drone in every direction to pick up its angle readings.<\/p>\n<p class=\"highlight-line\" data-source-line=\"58\">Enclose the code you wrote in a <strong>repeat<\/strong> block.<\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;10609&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]Sometimes, it can help to\u00a0format the output of your code so that it is easier for a human to read and interpret the data. In the &#8220;Input\/Output&#8221; menu, use the <strong>create text with<\/strong> block with print statements to format the output.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;8948&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]Finally, print the X, Y, and Z angles with a label. Add a space before and after the letter so you can read your output clearly.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;8949&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]When you run your code, your drone will not fly, so try picking it up and moving it in different directions slowly to see the numbers change. You will notice that sometimes the numbers are positive and sometimes they are negative. Tilting to the left, backward, or turning to the right will return negative angles. When you turn beyond 180 degrees, the value will switch to -180. <em>What else do you notice?<\/em>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221378246d4\" name=\"6a221378246d4\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Colorful Turns<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]Your mission is to program the CoDrone EDU to change its LED colors based on its turning direction. Each quadrant of the circle will be represented by a different color: Red for 0-90 degrees, Yellow for 90-180 degrees, Green for -180 to -90 degrees, and Blue for -90 to 0 degrees.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;8951&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<p class=\"\" data-source-line=\"78\">Here are the steps to help you get started.<\/p>\n<ul data-source-line=\"79\">\n<li class=\"\" data-source-line=\"79\">Create a loop that repeats 100 times<\/li>\n<li class=\"\" data-source-line=\"80\">Inside the loop, read the z-angle and store it in a variable<\/li>\n<li class=\"\" data-source-line=\"81\">Add an if statement with 3 else-if conditions<\/li>\n<li class=\"\" data-source-line=\"82\">In the first condition, change the color to red if the angle &gt;= 0 and angle &lt; 90.<\/li>\n<li class=\"\" data-source-line=\"83\">Repeat for the other 4 quadrants.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"highlight-line\" data-source-line=\"85\"><em>Note:\u00a0this program doesn\u2019t include any takeoff, landing, or flight commands! Place your drone flat and turn it with your hand. As a challenge, add in flight commands and have the LEDs change color while flying.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;12931&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22137824736\" name=\"6a22137824736\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 8<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Turn_degree()<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]We provide a function that allows the drone to turn to a given angle, <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">turn_degree()<\/code>.\u00a0 This function turns the drone right or left with absolute reference frame to drone&#8217;s initial heading. The drone&#8217;s initial heading is defined (set to 0 degrees) by the drone&#8217;s heading after takeoff or when drone pairs to the controller after powering on.[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]Using <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">turn_degree(45)<\/code> will turn the drone 45 degrees to the left, and using <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">turn_degree(-45)<\/code> will turn the drone 45 degrees to the right.[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;13362&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_single_image image=&#8221;13363&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]What happens if you run the code below?[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;13364&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]You will notice that using <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">turn_degree(45)<\/code> consecutively will turn the drone 45 degrees to the left, relative to the drone&#8217;s initial heading, once and then do nothing after. Why? This is because, after the drone turns +45 degrees for the first time, the drone is already +45 degrees relative to the drone&#8217;s initial heading! Therefore, the drone does not have to turn again.[\/vc_column_text][vc_column_text]Run this code to become familiar with the <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">turn_degree()<\/code> function:[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;13365&#8243; img_size=&#8221;large&#8221; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;][vc_column_text]<strong>Note: <\/strong>This function only accepts angles from -180 to 180 degrees.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22137824796\" name=\"6a22137824796\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 9<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge: A More Perfect Polygon<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<p class=\"\" data-source-line=\"91\"><em>Remember when you flew CoDrone EDU in different shapes?<\/em> Now, let&#8217;s put that knowledge to use with the gyroscope! Create programs that turn just the right amount of degrees for each shape. Here&#8217;s a tip: use <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">turn_degree()<\/code>.<\/p>\n<ul data-source-line=\"92\">\n<li class=\"\" data-source-line=\"92\">Triangle<\/li>\n<li class=\"\" data-source-line=\"93\">Square<\/li>\n<li class=\"\" data-source-line=\"94\">Pentagon<\/li>\n<li class=\"highlight-line\" data-source-line=\"95\">Hexagon<\/li>\n<li class=\"\" data-source-line=\"96\">Octagon<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221378247e6\" name=\"6a221378247e6\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 10<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Lesson Complete<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<div>\n<div>You&#8217;ve just unlocked the potential of the CoDrone EDU&#8217;s gyroscope sensor. Now you can use this tool to detect changes in the drone&#8217;s orientation, ensuring precise movements and correct alignment. Get ready to implement this knowledge and combine it with other sensors.<\/div>\n<\/div>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text][vc_single_image image=&#8221;7547&#8243; img_size=&#8221;750&#215;670&#8243; alignment=&#8221;center&#8221;]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 11 We've Moved! [vc_column_text css=\".vc_custom_1761251960983{padding-top: 20px !important;padding-right: 20px !important;padding-bottom: 20px !important;padding-left: 20px !important;background-color: #ffdddd !important;border-radius: 3px !important;}\"] We\u2019ve moved to our new lesson portal: Robolink Learn! Lessons here&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/3-4-gyroscope-cde-blockly\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":4742,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":3877,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1782,1848,1770,1793,1826,1482,853,694,59,171,63],"lesson-tag":[2195,2108,4328,14401,2162,2161,2122,2155],"class_list":["post-5913","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-advanced","lesson-tag-blockly","lesson-tag-cde","lesson-tag-cdej","lesson-tag-gyro","lesson-tag-gyroscope","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-sensor","module-advanced","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/5913","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":43,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/5913\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":13634,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/5913\/revisions\/13634"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/4742"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=5913"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=5913"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=5913"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}